menu "TC32 Options"
	depends on TC32

config ARCH
	string
	default "tc32"

config GEN_ISR_TABLES
	default y

config GEN_IRQ_VECTOR_TABLE
	default n

config GEN_SW_ISR_TABLE
	default y

config TC32_BOOT_MIRROR_SIZE
	hex "TC32 boot mirror SRAM reserve size"
	default 0x4000
	help
	  Size of the low SRAM window reserved for the TLSR boot-ROM flash
	  mirror. Under the current XIP startup flow this window must cover the
	  small reset/IRQ bootstrap prefix and the adjacent low-SRAM instruction
	  cache tag/data scratch area used by the TLSR827x flash execution path
	  before normal RAM placement begins.

config TC32_FAST_DIV64
	bool "64-bit division by 32-bit digits"
	default y
	help
	  The 64-bit divisions of the runtime (__aeabi_uldivmod,
	  __aeabi_ldivmod, __divdi3 and the others in
	  arch/tc32/core/compiler_builtins.c) divide by 32-bit steps: one
	  32-bit division when both operands fit in 32 bits, two or four
	  more for a 32-bit divisor (on the TLSR8278 its hardware divider),
	  and shift-and-subtract over the quotient's bits only for a larger
	  divisor; a call takes tens to a few hundred instructions. Without
	  it they divide bit by bit over all 64 bits of the dividend, a few
	  thousand instructions a call, with less code.
	  The bit-serial division is built either way as
	  __tc32_uldivmod_proven, for code that calls it by name; without
	  this option __aeabi_uldivmod is that function under another name.

config TC32_POP_PC_RETURNS
	bool "Thumb returns with pop {..., pc}"
	help
	  Every C compile gets the Thumb-only target feature noarm
	  (-Xclang -target-feature -Xclang +noarm): the code never returns
	  to ARM state, so a function that saved lr returns with
	  pop {..., pc} instead of pop {...}; pop {rN}; bx rN, a few bytes
	  less per function. A compile unit named with tc32_classic_returns()
	  (arch/tc32/CMakeLists.txt) keeps the bx returns. The configuration
	  stops when a test function compiled with the feature does not return
	  with pop {..., pc} (a clang without the feature ignores it without a
	  message), or compiled with +noarm then -noarm does not return
	  through bx. On the direct path (TC32_DIRECT, -mcpu=tc32) the core
	  has the feature itself; without this option every C compile there
	  gets -noarm, so that both paths give the same code.

choice TC32_ICF
	prompt "Identical code folding in the link"
	default TC32_ICF_NONE
	depends on LLVM_USE_LLD
	help
	  ld.lld's --icf keeps one copy of input sections with the same
	  contents, relocations and flags, and points every reference to the
	  others at it. It folds only sections that the linker script puts in
	  the same output section, so code in .ram_code, .boot_ram_mirror or
	  .tc32_flash_copy never merges with code in flash. Folded symbols share
	  an address, so a symbolizer or an emulator breakpoint on one of them
	  names or stops at both.

config TC32_ICF_NONE
	bool "None"

config TC32_ICF_SAFE
	bool "Safe: sections whose address nothing uses"
	help
	  ld.lld --icf=safe folds code and read-only data only when no object
	  marks a symbol of the section address-significant: clang lists every
	  symbol whose address the code uses other than in a direct call in the
	  object's .llvm_addrsig table, and from an object without that
	  table, the translated assembly sources for example, every symbol it
	  defines or references counts as address-significant.
	  Two functions that code compares through pointers keep different
	  addresses.

config TC32_ICF_ALL
	bool "All: code even when its address is used"
	help
	  ld.lld --icf=all folds identical code sections whether or not their
	  address is used: two different functions can then have the same
	  address, so code that tells handlers, callbacks or API functions
	  apart by comparing pointers can take one for the other. Read-only
	  data keeps the safe rule.

endchoice

endmenu
